course: dictionary
lesson: 1883
title: Collectivisation: a hard word from history
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Collectivisation is a long word, so break it into pieces. Collective means shared by a group, and the ending -isation turns it into a process, something that is being done. Collectivisation means putting things that belonged to separate people under shared or state ownership.
explain: In history it has a strong meaning. «forced collectivisation (making farmers give their land and animals to big state farms)» describes what happened in the Soviet Union in the late 1920s and 1930s. The word forced is a clue: the farmers did not choose it.
explain: Read the words around it for more clues. The sentence also mentions «impossible grain quotas (amounts each village had to hand over)» and seized food. Those clues show that collectivisation was not a gentle sharing of land. Always ask: does my meaning fit the whole story?
fact: Collectivisation means forcing farmers to give up their own land and animals and join big farms run by the state.
fact: In the Soviet Union, the big shared farms were called collective farms, or kolkhozes.
fact: The famine of 1932–1933 in Soviet Ukraine was caused by Soviet policy, including forced collectivisation, and millions of people died.
fact: Ukraine and many other countries call this famine the Holodomor and recognise it as a genocide, which means an attempt to destroy a people. Some historians debate the legal label.
fact: China also collectivised its farms in the 1950s.
exercise: In history, what does collectivisation mean? | Farmers choosing to buy the biggest tractors in their village | A holiday on a farm for city children | *Forcing farmers to give up their land and animals and join big state farms | Planting trees in a town park
exercise: Which word in «forced collectivisation» tells you the farmers did not choose it? | state | *forced | land | Farmers
exercise: What does genocide mean? | A kind of grain | A large family farm | A famous Soviet painting | *An attempt to destroy a people
test: Soviet collective farms were also called what? | Villages | Quotas | *Kolkhozes | Granaries
test: Which country also collectivised its farms in the 1950s? | Brazil | Egypt | *China | Norway
exam: The lesson says «forced collectivisation» and that farmers who resisted were punished. What does this tell you? | It was a happy festival for farmers | *It was done against people's will, not chosen by them | It made everyone rich very quickly | It only happened in one small village
